Limitations
The algorithm cannot handle graphs with negative weight cycles for 
shortest path calculations, as the distances keep decreasing indefinitely. However, it can detect such cycles efficiently.
The Bellman-Ford algorithm is a single-source shortest path algorithm that works on graphs with negative edge weights. 
It is particularly useful for detecting negative weight cycles. The runtime of the algorithm depends on the number of 
vertices (V) and edges (E) in the graph.

Time Complexity
The Bellman-Ford algorithm has a time complexity of O(V * E) in the worst case. This is because the algorithm performs (V - 1) iterations 
of edge relaxation, where each iteration processes all edges in the graph.
Worst Case: O(V * E). This occurs when the graph requires all (V - 1) iterations to compute the shortest paths, such as in cases with complex edge arrangements or near-negative cycles.
Best Case: O(E). If the shortest paths are found in the first iteration and no further updates are needed, the algorithm can terminate early.
Average Case: O(V * E). On average, the algorithm processes all edges for (V - 1) iterations.

Space Complexity
The space complexity of the Bellman-Ford algorithm is O(V). 
This is because it maintains a distance array to store the shortest path estimates for each vertex.

Key Factors Affecting Runtime
Number of Edges (E): The runtime scales linearly with the number of edges processed in each iteration.
Number of Vertices (V): The algorithm performs (V - 1) iterations of edge relaxation.
Graph Structure: Sparse graphs (fewer edges) result in faster execution compared to dense graphs.

Practical Considerations
The Bellman-Ford algorithm is slower than Dijkstra's algorithm for graphs without negative weights due to its O(V * E) complexity. 
However, it is preferred when negative weights are present or when detecting negative weight cycles is required.
